{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"3bbd2712741cf5815fe3f058809283e36dce3f9c4000237579561fdd6d214331"},{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed 'reduction' is circular: SRC_002 says the isolation 'reduces to' the empirical observation that the artifact has no accepted relations — but that observation IS the isolation itself. A genuine reduction must explain the isolated state in terms of something more fundamental (a mechanism, a generating process, a lower-level description), not merely relabel the same fact as 'data.' From a causal-mechanism stance, no mechanism is identified that produces or grounds the isolation; the target restates the endpoint (isolation) as an empirical measurement and calls that a reduction, which is a summary of the endpoint, not a substantive relation.","target":"66de59094a4954cfda5537e2fce4d54679d1bf1ffe6a5f54be95de588b589fb6"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"a06b7d946cc321c994a671aa08fb1fdb080f07b04f2e5ff3baf716b489377e42"},{"attack":true,"case":"The contradiction rests on an asserted causal claim — that forced mechanism-sharing connections 'destroy the very signal that isolation provides' — with no causal mechanism identified. From the demand-a-causal-mechanism stance, the target must show HOW a forced connection in one part of the artifact graph causally undermines the diagnostic value of isolation elsewhere. Instead it simply posits epistemic incompatibility as self-evident. The refutation condition itself concedes the two might coexist, undermining the claim that this is a genuine contradiction rather than a tentative disagreement about epistemic values.","target":"e59dbcce7e3244d7d8ca69740a8b7ba2e3e5191b8ba3da5a5fa3505e6ef04596"}]}